/// \defgroup grp_w1023b_gameclient_input_credits_sway Credits roll, tree sway and input translators
/// \ingroup grp_gameclient
/// \brief The credits roll, the wind sway of drawables, three of the message
///        stream's translators and the selection-versus-command decision:
///        client-side presentation and input handling.
///
/// Everything here runs on the local machine on the client side. The bodies
/// documented here do not step the simulation, and the credits roll and the
/// sway module make no logic random draws; the window callbacks and UI hooks
/// that these bodies call are outside this group. The input translators
/// decide which messages reach the command list, so they shape what the local
/// player orders. The pieces fall into two kinds.
///
/// \par Presentation
/// - CreditsManager is the scrolling credits roll of the main menu. It reads
///   `Credits.ini`, so the roll's text, order, styles and speed are data.
/// - SwayClientUpdate is a client update module that rocks trees and similar
///   drawables in the wind that map scripts set.
///
/// \par Input: the message-stream translators
/// Keyboard and Mouse append raw messages to `TheMessageStream` every client
/// frame (\ref sub_gameclient_input). MessageStream::propagateMessages then
/// gives each attached translator one complete pass over the stream, lowest
/// priority number first; a translator keeps a message, destroys it, or
/// appends new ones, and what survives all of them is handed to the command
/// list in one call (\ref page_core_frameworks_messages). GameClient::init
/// attaches the translators and fixes their order:
/// - priority 4: WindowTranslator, in mode 0;
/// - priority 5: AptTranslator, the Apt UI's mouse translator;
/// - priority 10: WindowTranslator, in mode 1;
/// - priority 20: MetaEventTranslator, raw keys to bound commands and raw
///   buttons to click messages;
/// - priorities 25, 30, 35, 50 and 70: HotKeyTranslator, PlaceEventTranslator,
///   FormationTranslator, SelectionTranslator and the command translator,
///   with further translators at priorities 27, 40 and 60;
/// - priority 100: HintSpyTranslator, which reports hints and orders to the UI;
/// - the largest priority value: GameClientMessageDispatcher, the final stop.
///
/// Zero Hour attaches ten translators, with one window translator and no Apt
/// or formation translator; the numbers above are BFME 2's. This group
/// documents WindowTranslator, MetaEventTranslator and HintSpyTranslator,
/// the helper rawMouseToWindowMessage, and contextCommandForNewSelection,
/// which the selection translator uses to decide whether a click selects or
/// commands. It does not describe how the logic executes the resulting
/// commands.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Key bindings are data (`CommandMap.ini`; see MetaEventTranslator). The
/// translator chain, its priorities and the rules of each translator are
/// fixed in code. The credits roll and the sway module make no logic random
/// draws (sway draws only from the client random stream). The translators are
/// different: they decide which messages reach the command list, so changing
/// their rules changes which orders a player can issue.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The translator chain is the main input seam of the client: the raw
/// producers (Keyboard, Mouse) append raw messages, and the translators
/// (window systems, selection, commands, the UI hints) consume them from the
/// stream, so a new input backend replaces the raw producers. The seam is not
/// clean. Translators also read client objects directly (for example
/// MetaEventTranslator reads the drag tolerance from the mouse object, and
/// WindowTranslator reads the in-game UI and the tactical view), and the UI
/// hooks that HintSpyTranslator calls are also called by other code, including
/// SelectionTranslator and a window callback (see HintSpyTranslator). Raw
/// keys are DirectInput scan codes and mouse
/// positions are integer pixels. Several
/// rules (bindings whose `UseableIn` is exactly GAME do not fire while the
/// client frame is still 0, the German Y/Z swap, the drag tolerance) are
/// class details listed on the class pages.
///
/// \see \ref sub_gameclient

/// \class CreditsManager
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gameclient_input_credits_sway
/// \brief The credits roll: styled text lines read from Credits.ini and
///        scrolled across the screen, one new line at a time.
///
/// A CreditsManager holds everything for one run of the credits screen. It
/// keeps an ordered list of every line the INI file defines, a shorter list
/// of the lines that are on screen right now, the scroll settings, and a
/// marker that says which line of the ordered list comes next. Each line (a
/// `CreditsLine`) has a style, one or two pieces of text and, while it is on
/// screen, a display string, a position, a height and a colour. The object is
/// a subsystem-style class (a virtual destructor plus init, load, reset and
/// update entries), but it is not one of the subsystems that
/// GameEngine::init registers: the main menu creates and drives it directly,
/// as described under Ownership and lifetime.
///
/// \par Line styles
/// - TITLE, MINORTITLE and NORMAL are one centred line of text each, drawn
///   in the title, minor-title or normal credits font and the matching colour.
///   The code calls MINORTITLE the "position" style.
/// - COLUMN is a line of two texts, drawn centred on one third and on two
///   thirds of the width. The first COLUMN text starts a line and the next
///   one completes it (see CreditsManager::addText). It uses the normal font
///   and colour.
/// - BLANK is a spacer as tall as one line of the normal font. The style has
///   no name in the INI file; the `Blank` field adds it.
///
/// \par Credits.ini
/// CreditsManager::load reads `Data\INI\Credits.ini`. The fields of a block
/// are:
/// - `ScrollRate`: pixels moved per scroll step.
/// - `ScrollRateEveryFrames`: how many calls of CreditsManager::update pass
///   between two scroll steps.
/// - `ScrollDown`: yes if lines enter at the top and move down, no if they
///   enter at the bottom and move up.
/// - `TitleColor`, `MinorTitleColor` and `NormalColor`: the three text
///   colours.
/// - `Style`: sets the style for the `Text` entries that follow it. It is a
///   running setting, not a per-line argument, so the order of the lines in
///   the file matters. The names are TITLE, MINORTITLE, NORMAL and COLUMN.
/// - `Blank`: appends a spacer line.
/// - `Text`: appends a line from one quoted string, in the current style. The
///   string is either a string-table label or literal text; see "Text lookup"
///   below.
///
/// Fields act in file order as the parser reads them, which is why a `Style`
/// line takes effect for the `Text` lines below it. Before any `Style` line
/// the style is NORMAL. Colours start white, and both scroll settings start at
/// 1 with scrolling downward, as in Zero Hour.
///
/// \par Text lookup (private getUnicodeString)
/// The string of a `Text` entry is turned into display text by the private
/// member `getUnicodeString`, which only CreditsManager::addText calls. This
/// guide is built with EXTRACT_PRIVATE=NO, so that member has no entry of its
/// own and its rules are given here. It takes the raw string from the INI file and returns the
/// Unicode text to show. There are three cases, checked in this order:
/// - The string is exactly `<BLANK>` (compared case-sensitively): the result
///   is the shared empty string.
/// - The string contains a colon anywhere: it is treated as a
///   localisation label and looked up in `TheGameText`. A label that the
///   loaded string tables do not know gives a "missing string" placeholder.
///   In BFME 2, GameTextManager::fetchPtr builds that text as "MISSING:"
///   followed by the label in single quotes; when the text manager's
///   translate mode is 1, that function instead returns the label converted
///   to Unicode.
/// - Otherwise it is literal text, converted by UnicodeString::translate,
///   which decodes UTF-8 to UTF-16 in BFME 2.
///
/// The conversion happens when the line is added, so the string table must
/// already be loaded when `Credits.ini` is read.
///
/// Modding: the colon test is the whole rule. A literal credit that happens
/// to contain a colon (for example "Music: Someone") is looked up as a label
/// and most likely shows as missing text; put such a line in the string table
/// and refer to it by label instead. A literal line with non-ASCII characters
/// must be UTF-8 in `Credits.ini`.
///
/// Provenance: the three cases are Zero Hour's code, and the body matches it
/// in BFME 2. The `MISSING` detail and the UTF-8 decoding are from the BFME 2
/// bodies of GameTextManager::fetchPtr and UnicodeString::translate. BFME 2's
/// text manager declares `fetch` overloads next to the `fetchPtr` overloads,
/// and the credits call is written as `TheGameText->fetch(str)`. Whether that
/// call forwards to fetchPtr is open, so the placeholder text and the
/// translate-mode behaviour above are those of fetchPtr and may not be exactly
/// what the credits call returns. Zero Hour has no fetchPtr, and its `fetch`
/// taking an AsciiString calls the `fetch` taking a C string. The
/// empty-string constant is the shared one of UnicodeString.
///
/// \par Where the fonts come from
/// The three credits fonts are not in this file. They are the
/// `CreditsTitleFont`, `CreditsMinorTitleFont` and `CreditsNormalFont`
/// fields of the `Language` INI block (name, size and bold flag each),
/// which `GlobalLanguage` holds. Sizes pass through
/// GlobalLanguage::adjustFontSize, which scales a point size by the
/// horizontal resolution over 1024.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// A roll is created for each visit to the credits screen:
/// - AptMainMenu::Credits (the main menu's Credits callback) deletes any
///   earlier roll, creates a new manager, calls its load entry and then its
///   init entry, starts the credits music and the "MainMenuToCreditsScreen"
///   transition, puts the menu into its credits state and raises the
///   engine's frame-rate cap to 100. That is the order of Zero Hour's
///   credits menu (load, then init). Zero Hour's init entry clears the
///   finished flag, sets the next-line marker to the first line of the
///   ordered list and zeroes the frame counter, which is why load has to come
///   first there. BFME 2's update only compares and advances the marker, and
///   BFME 2's load does not set it, so the marker is set elsewhere; that the
///   second entry does so, as Zero Hour's init does, is an inference from the
///   call order.
/// - While the menu is in its credits state, its per-frame state machine
///   calls CreditsManager::update and, once the manager reports that it has
///   finished, asks the Apt layer to run its `HideCredits` function.
/// - AptMainMenu::RenderCredits (an Apt callback bound by name) calls the
///   roll's draw pass if a roll exists. The draw pass receives an origin and
///   a size from the Apt layer, stores the size in the manager, then draws
///   every line on screen. It fades a line by its position: fully opaque in
///   the middle third of the height, fading to nothing towards the top and
///   bottom edges. COLUMN lines are drawn at one third and two thirds of the
///   width. Zero Hour's name for this method is draw.
/// - AptMainMenu::CreditsExit resets the roll, deletes it, and restores the
///   frame-rate cap from the global data.
///
/// The ordered list owns its lines; the destructor deletes them, and a line
/// frees its display strings when it is destroyed. The on-screen list only
/// refers to lines the ordered list owns.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: the text, order, styles, colours and speed of the roll
///   (`Credits.ini`, opened through the file system, so a BIG archive or a
///   loose file can replace it); the three fonts (the `Language` block);
///   and the wording of any line that uses a string-table label.
/// - Hard-coded: the four named styles plus the spacer, the two-column
///   layout at one third and two thirds, the fade zones, the 2-pixel gap
///   added between lines when scrolling upward, and the colon test that
///   separates labels from literal text.
/// - The file is loaded without a checksum writer, so it does not feed the
///   INI CRC that \ref page_modding_rules describes. This is read from
///   CreditsManager::load, which passes no such object to INI::load.
/// - The roll runs in the main menu, outside any game, so no simulation,
///   save or replay state is involved.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - Positions are integer pixels inside the rectangle that the draw pass
///   stores. CreditsManager::update does nothing until the first draw has
///   supplied a positive size, so the roll adapts to whatever area the
///   Apt layer reports. The font sizes, however, scale with the game's
///   horizontal resolution, not with that rectangle.
/// - The scroll speed is "pixels per step, one step per N update calls". It
///   follows the rate at which the main menu is updated, not elapsed time.
///   The main menu raises the frame-rate cap to 100 while the roll plays and
///   restores it afterwards, so the on-screen speed depends on that cap; a
///   port that changes the update rate must scale ScrollRate or the step
///   interval.
/// - Text is drawn through display strings from `TheDisplayStringManager`,
///   which is the seam for a different text renderer.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Facts from BFME 2: the field table (tokens and the four style names), the
/// bodies of the functions documented below, the main menu callbacks that
/// create, update, draw and delete the roll, and the manager's
/// destructor and constructor defaults. Facts carried from Zero Hour (its
/// `Credits.cpp` and `CreditsMenu.cpp`): the role of `Style`, `Text` and
/// `Blank`, and the draw pass's name. Zero Hour and Open-BFME-1 both read the
/// width and height from the display object on each call; the stored-size
/// guard is specific to the BFME 2 body. Inference: the meaning of the
/// origin and size arguments of the draw pass is read from its body, not from
/// a name.
/// \see CreditsManager::load, CreditsManager::update, CreditsManager::addText,
///      AptMainMenu::Credits, AptMainMenu::CreditsExit,
///      AptMainMenu::RenderCredits


/// \fn bool CreditsManager::load()
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gameclient_input_credits_sway
/// \brief Reads Credits.ini into the manager and measures the normal font's
///        line height.
///
/// The first of the two calls that make a roll ready; CreditsManager is
/// created, loaded and then initialised by AptMainMenu::Credits.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Creates a local INI parser and loads `Data\INI\Credits.ini` in
///   overwrite mode. No checksum object is passed. The fourth argument of
///   INI::load is INI::parseCredits, so every block of the file goes to that
///   callback, which applies the manager's field table to the roll with
///   INI::initFromINI. INI::load does not examine the word that opens a
///   block before calling the callback, so that word is not validated. Each
///   `Text` field reaches CreditsManager::addText as it is read, and each
///   `Blank` field adds a spacer line.
/// - Raises the two scroll settings to at least 1 if the file left them at
///   zero or below.
/// - Looks up the normal credits font through `TheFontLibrary` (name, size
///   scaled by GlobalLanguage::adjustFontSize, bold flag) and keeps its pixel
///   height. A spacer line is this tall.
/// - Returns true.
///
/// \return Always true. Zero Hour's version returns nothing.
///
/// \warning It appends lines and clears nothing, so loading twice into the
/// same manager repeats every line. The main menu creates a new manager for
/// each roll. The body reads the language data and the font library without
/// testing them for null.
/// \see CreditsManager::addText, CreditsManager::update


/// \fn void CreditsManager::addText(AsciiString text)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gameclient_input_credits_sway
/// \brief Appends one credits text entry, using the style that the INI file
///        has selected so far.
///
/// The INI callback for the `Text` field reads the next quoted string and
/// passes it here. The
/// text is converted to Unicode as it is added, not when it is displayed, so
/// the string table must already be loaded when `Credits.ini` is read.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// A new line object is made first. What happens next depends on the current
/// style:
/// - TITLE, MINORTITLE or NORMAL: the line's text is the argument converted
///   to Unicode (the "Text lookup" rules on the CreditsManager page), its
///   style is the current style, and it goes on the end of the ordered list.
/// - COLUMN: the last line of the ordered list is examined. If the list is
///   empty, or that line is not a COLUMN line, or it already has both texts,
///   the new line becomes a COLUMN line holding the first text and is marked
///   as waiting for a second one. Otherwise the text becomes the second text
///   of that last line, the line is marked complete, and the spare line
///   object is deleted. Column entries therefore pair up in order: first with
///   second, third with fourth. An odd entry at the end leaves a line with
///   only a left column.
/// - Any other style value: the spare line is deleted and nothing is added.
///   The INI file cannot select such a style. Zero Hour raises a debug
///   assertion here; a release build, as in this code, just discards the
///   line.
///
/// \param text A string-table label, literal text, or `<BLANK>`; see the
///             "Text lookup" rules on the CreditsManager page.
///
/// \note The spacer style is not available to this function. Spacer lines
/// come from the `Blank` field.
/// \see CreditsManager, CreditsManager::load


/// \fn void CreditsManager::update()
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gameclient_input_credits_sway
/// \brief Advances the roll by one step: scrolls the lines on screen,
///        retires the ones that have left, and starts the next line when the
///        newest one has fully entered.
///
/// Called once per main-menu update while the menu is in its credits state.
/// It never draws; the draw pass reads the positions that this function
/// leaves behind.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Returns at once if the roll has finished or if the stored width or
///   height is not positive. Those sizes are written by the draw pass, so the
///   roll does not start, and its frame counter does not advance, until the
///   first draw has happened. Zero Hour reads the size from the display
///   instead.
/// - Counts the call, and does the rest only on every
///   `ScrollRateEveryFrames`-th call.
/// - Moves every line on screen by `ScrollRate` pixels (down when scrolling
///   down, otherwise up). A line is retired when it has passed the far
///   edge: below the bottom edge when scrolling down, or, when scrolling up,
///   once its bottom edge plus the 2-pixel gap is above the top. Retiring
///   frees the line's display strings and removes it from the on-screen list;
///   the line stays in the ordered list.
/// - The newest line, the last one examined, decides whether to start another.
///   Scrolling down, it must have reached the top edge; scrolling up, its
///   bottom edge plus a 2-pixel gap must have risen to the bottom of the
///   area. If it has not, the call ends.
/// - Once the newest line has made room: if nothing is left on screen and the
///   next-line marker is at the end of the ordered list, the roll is marked
///   finished. If the marker is at the end, the call then ends; any lines
///   still on screen scroll off over the following calls.
/// - Otherwise it prepares the next line of the ordered list. It sets the
///   colour for the line's style; then, for a style that has text, if the
///   language data exists and the text is not empty, it takes a new display
///   string from `TheDisplayStringManager` (and gives up for this call if
///   none is available), sets the credits font for the style, sets the text,
///   measures it, and centres the line horizontally on the stored width. The
///   line's height is the measured height. Its first position is just
///   outside the area on the entering side. A COLUMN line does this for both
///   texts; its stored horizontal position is overwritten by the second
///   text's, and the draw pass ignores that field for COLUMN lines. A spacer
///   line gets the normal font's height and no display string.
/// - The prepared line is added to the on-screen list and the next-line
///   marker moves on. At most one line starts per call.
///
/// \note Zero Hour and Open-BFME-1 ask the display object for the width and
/// height each time; BFME 2 uses the sizes stored by the draw pass.
/// \note A line with empty text, or one prepared when there is no language
/// data, gets no display string but is still added to the on-screen list and
/// consumed from the ordered list.
///
/// \par Modding
/// `ScrollRate`, `ScrollRateEveryFrames` and `ScrollDown` in `Credits.ini`
/// control the motion; the 2-pixel gap and the "newest line has fully
/// entered" rule are fixed.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Positions and speeds are integers in pixels, advanced per call, not per
/// second. See CreditsManager for the frame-rate cap that the main menu sets
/// while the roll runs.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The algorithm is Zero Hour's `CreditsManager::update`; the stored-size
/// guard and use of stored sizes are what the BFME 2 body does differently,
/// and are read from that body. The display-string calls go through the
/// engine's display string manager and its font library as in Zero Hour.
/// \see CreditsManager::load, CreditsManager::addText


/// \class SwayClientUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gameclient_input_credits_sway
/// \brief A client update module that makes a drawable, in practice a tree or
///        other foliage-like object, rock to and fro in the wind.
///
/// SwayClientUpdate is one of the client update modules: small per-drawable
/// objects whose clientUpdate runs once per client frame (Zero Hour's
/// arrangement) and which only affect what is shown. The module factory registers it under the name
/// `SwayClientUpdate` as a client update. An `Object` block attaches it with
/// a `ClientUpdate = SwayClientUpdate <tag>` line (the thing template's field
/// table has a `ClientUpdate` keyword that parses a module name of the
/// client-update type). Zero Hour's module has no fields of its own; whether
/// BFME 2's has any is not established.
///
/// The module does not own the wind. The wind is the "breeze", a few
/// numbers held by the script engine (direction, sway amount, lean, period,
/// randomness and a version counter). The module reads them and turns them
/// into a rocking motion:
/// - Each client update advances a phase, and the tilt it wants is
///   `sin(phase) * amplitude + lean`. The module rotates the drawable's
///   instance matrix by the difference between that and the tilt it applied
///   last time, so the tilt accumulates in the matrix. The instance matrix is
///   the drawable's local transform, which Zero Hour concatenates with the
///   global one. The rotation is about the drawable's local X and Y axes,
///   weighted by the sine and cosine of the wind direction.
/// - Amplitude, phase step and lean are the breeze values multiplied by a
///   random factor in the range 1 minus half the randomness to 1 plus half the
///   randomness, drawn separately for each drawable so that neighbours do not
///   move in step. With a randomness of zero the phase is also reset, and all
///   drawables sway together.
/// - When the breeze version number differs from the one the module cached,
///   the module recomputes its values at once, even if the drawable is
///   off screen, so that nothing jumps when it comes into view. Otherwise a
///   drawable that is not visible is skipped and its phase does not advance.
/// - Two conditions are checked in SwayClientUpdate::clientUpdate: if the
///   drawable's object has the status bit that BFME 1's status name table
///   calls BURNED, or is effectively dead, the module clears its swaying flag
///   and does not sway again; the save stores that flag. The BFME 2 body passes
///   a fixed status number to the object's status test; the name BURNED for it
///   is carried from BFME 1 (and Zero Hour), and BFME 2's own status
///   numbering is not independently confirmed here.
/// - Those two are not the only way the flag is cleared. When a topple starts
///   (ToppleUpdate::applyTopplingForce, for example a felled tree), that
///   function finds each client module named `SwayClientUpdate` on the
///   object's drawable and stops its sway. Zero Hour: ToppleUpdate calls
///   SwayClientUpdate::stopSway. BFME 2: ToppleUpdate clears the swaying flag
///   directly, with the same effect as stopSway. It does this after its early
///   returns, so it does not happen for an object that is already
///   effectively dead (which clientUpdate stops anyway) or when the topple
///   data kills the object at the start.
///
/// \par What BFME 2 changes from Zero Hour
/// The structure is Zero Hour's, and Open-BFME-1's version has the same
/// changes. Read from the BFME 2 bodies:
/// - The wave is a sine where Zero Hour's is a cosine. The module's phase
///   starts at zero, so a drawable now begins at the lean angle and swings
///   out, instead of starting at full amplitude.
/// - The wind direction is taken relative to the drawable's own facing (the
///   breeze direction minus the Z rotation of its transform) and kept as a
///   sine and cosine in two extra members. Zero Hour applies the breeze's
///   world-space direction to the local instance matrix unchanged, so there
///   a turned drawable does not rock along the wind direction (an inference
///   from the two bodies).
/// - The phase step is a full turn divided by the breeze period multiplied
///   by a value read from the game engine. Zero Hour divides by the period
///   alone.
/// - The BFME 2 clientUpdate body also stops the sway for an effectively dead
///   object. Zero Hour's clientUpdate tests only the status bit named BURNED
///   there. (For BFME 2 the bit is identified by the number the body passes,
///   matched to BFME 1's name table; see Provenance.)
/// - The instance-matrix setter takes a second boolean, which this module
///   passes as false; what the flag controls is not established.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// A drawable creates its client modules through the module factory when it
/// is built from its thing template, and they live as long as it does (this
/// is Zero Hour's arrangement; BFME 2's factory registration and creation
/// stub follow the same pattern). The constructor starts with every motion
/// value at zero, the swaying flag set and the cached breeze version at -1,
/// which no breeze ever has. It does no sway work itself: the first client
/// update sees the version mismatch and computes the real values.
///
/// \par Saves
/// The module saves its own members in a version-1 block: the five motion
/// values, the cached version, the swaying flag, and (not in Zero Hour) the
/// two direction values, without a version bump. A light CRC pass skips
/// the module's own members. After a load, SwayClientUpdate::loadPostProcess
/// runs SwayClientUpdate::updateSway again, so the random factors and
/// the facing-relative direction are recomputed from the breeze that the
/// script engine saved.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which objects sway (the `ClientUpdate` line in their
///   definitions) and what the wind is. The wind comes from the map-script
///   action "Set wind sway amount and direction" (internal name
///   SET_TREE_SWAY), whose five parameters are the direction, the amount to
///   sway, the amount to lean with the wind, the number of "frames to take to
///   sway once" and the randomness (0 for lock step, 1 for large variation).
///   ScriptEngine::setSway stores them, clamps the period to at least 1 and
///   increments the version. ScriptEngine::reset sets the defaults: a
///   direction of one third of pi radians, a sway amount of 0.035 times a
///   quarter of pi radians, a lean of 0.02 times a quarter of pi radians, a
///   randomness of 0.2 and a period of ten times the logic frame rate. Zero
///   Hour's defaults differ.
/// - The W3D device layer's vegetation buffer reads the same breeze structure
///   and rebuilds its own sway tables when the version changes (Zero Hour's
///   tree buffer does the same with the same structure), so one script action
///   moves objects and foliage together.
/// - Hard-coded: the waveform, the randomness rule, the cut-offs in
///   clientUpdate for objects with the burned-named status bit or an
///   effectively dead state, and the way the period is converted to a phase
///   step. A drawable's sway also stops when a ToppleUpdate module of its
///   object starts a topple (see the class description for the exact
///   conditions).
/// - The breeze is saved with the script engine (ScriptEngine::DoXfer), so a
///   loaded game keeps the wind its scripts set.
/// - It is presentation only. It draws its random factors from the client
///   random stream, not the logic one, so it does not change the order or
///   count of logic draws. The module writes only its own members and the
///   drawable's instance matrix.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The motion is advanced per client update, not per elapsed second, and
///   only while visible. The retail code divides the period by a value read
///   from the game engine object; the purpose of that divisor is not
///   established. The source's accessor for it is named as a
///   frames-per-second getter, but the network timing notes
///   (`docs/bfme2-network-timing-path.md`) document the member at that
///   position as a client-frame counter derived by dividing the FPS global by
///   the logic-tick global, which is a client-to-logic frame ratio rather
///   than a frames-per-second figure. What it holds at run time is therefore
///   not settled here; check it before changing the client frame rate or the
///   logic-to-client ratio.
/// - The tilt is applied as a difference to whatever the instance matrix
///   holds, so any other code that rewrites the instance matrix between two
///   updates changes the visible tilt without the module noticing. A renderer
///   that wants an absolute pose per frame would compute the pose from
///   `sin(phase) * amplitude + lean` directly. This is an inference from the
///   bookkeeping, not an observed fault.
/// - The wind direction relative to the facing is sampled when the values
///   are recomputed, not every frame.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Facts from BFME 2: the bodies and the save routine, the module factory
/// registration, the `SET_TREE_SWAY` action template, ScriptEngine::setSway
/// and ScriptEngine::reset, and the part of the body of
/// ToppleUpdate::applyTopplingForce that clears the swaying flag directly (the
/// member it clears is the one SwayClientUpdate::stopSway clears, read from
/// the two bodies). Facts carried from Zero Hour: the module's role and the
/// meaning of the members (its `SwayClientUpdate.h` and `.cpp`), and the
/// toppling function's call of SwayClientUpdate::stopSway. Also carried from
/// the donors, not established from BFME 2: the name BURNED for the status
/// number the body tests (BFME 1's status name table and Zero Hour's status
/// enumeration; the BFME 2 body passes only the number, and BFME 2's own
/// status numbering is not independently confirmed).
/// The source's name for the rate member (a frames-per-second getter) is its
/// own and is treated as unconfirmed; the network timing notes describe the
/// member differently (see Remastering).
/// \see SwayClientUpdate::updateSway, SwayClientUpdate::clientUpdate,
///      ScriptEngine::setSway, ScriptEngine::reset


/// \fn void SwayClientUpdate::updateSway()
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gameclient_input_credits_sway
/// \brief Recomputes this module's sway parameters from the current breeze
///        and the drawable's facing.
///
/// Called from SwayClientUpdate::clientUpdate when the breeze version has
/// changed (this includes the very first update) and from the module's
/// post-load step after a save is loaded. Nothing else calls it, and the
/// constructor does not.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Reads the breeze from the script engine. If its randomness is exactly
///   zero, the phase is reset to zero so that every drawable starts a cycle
///   together. Otherwise the phase is left where it is.
/// - With a spread equal to half the randomness, makes three draws from the
///   client random stream, each uniform in the range 1 minus the spread to 1
///   plus the spread:
///   - amplitude = the breeze's intensity times the first draw;
///   - phase step = a full turn divided by (the engine's rate value times
///     the breeze period), times the second draw. At the shipped rates, with
///     the breeze period that ScriptEngine::reset sets as its default, the
///     arithmetic gives one cycle in ten seconds at 30 client updates a
///     second if the rate value is the ratio of client frames to logic
///     frames; this is a calculation from the defaults, not an observed
///     result;
///   - lean = the breeze's lean times the third draw.
/// - Caches the breeze version number, so clientUpdate can tell when the
///   settings change again.
/// - If the module has a drawable, takes the breeze direction minus the
///   Z rotation of the drawable's transform matrix, and stores the sine and
///   cosine of that angle. clientUpdate uses them to split the tilt between
///   the two local axes. Without a drawable these stay as they were.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's version stops after caching the version number and
/// divides the period only by itself; the facing-relative direction is a
/// BFME 2 addition.
/// \note The draws come from the client stream (GameClientRandomValueReal),
/// so they never touch the logic stream.
/// \see SwayClientUpdate::clientUpdate, ScriptEngine::setSway


/// \fn void SwayClientUpdate::clientUpdate()
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gameclient_input_credits_sway
/// \brief Per-client-frame step of the sway: advances the phase and tilts the
///        drawable by the change since the last step.
///
/// Zero Hour's drawable update calls it once per client frame for each client
/// update module.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Returns at once if the swaying flag is clear.
/// - Compares the breeze version with the cached one. If they differ, runs
///   SwayClientUpdate::updateSway whether or not the drawable is visible.
///   Otherwise, if there is no drawable or it is not visible, returns without
///   advancing anything.
/// - Adds the phase step to the phase and wraps it past a full turn by one
///   subtraction.
/// - Computes the target tilt, the sine of the phase times the amplitude plus
///   the lean, and the difference from the tilt applied last time.
/// - Copies the drawable's instance matrix, pre-rotates the copy about the X
///   axis by minus the difference times the stored direction sine and about
///   the Y axis by the difference times the stored direction cosine, and
///   hands it back with the drawable's instance-matrix setter. Remembers the
///   target as the applied tilt.
/// - If the drawable has an object and that object has the status bit that
///   BFME 1's name table calls BURNED (BFME 2's own status numbering is not
///   independently confirmed here) or is effectively dead, clears the
///   swaying flag. The tilt step of the current call has already been
///   applied; later calls return at the top. These are the two conditions
///   this function checks; ToppleUpdate::applyTopplingForce also clears the
///   flag from outside (see SwayClientUpdate).
///
/// \note Zero Hour reads the world-space direction from the breeze and uses
/// a cosine; see SwayClientUpdate for the full list of differences.
/// \warning The body uses the drawable after the version-change branch
/// without testing it for null. A module always has a drawable in normal
/// use.
/// \see SwayClientUpdate::updateSway, Object::isEffectivelyDead


/// \class HintSpyTranslator
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gameclient_input_credits_sway
/// \brief The last UI-facing translator in the message stream: it turns hint
///        and order messages into feedback in the in-game UI, and eats the
///        hint messages.
///
/// A translator is an object with one job, translateGameMessage: it is
/// offered each message in the stream and answers KEEP_MESSAGE (leave it for
/// later translators and, in the end, the command list) or DESTROY_MESSAGE
/// (remove it). GameClient::init attaches the HintSpyTranslator at priority
/// 100. MessageStream::propagateMessages lets each translator make one
/// complete pass over the stream, in ascending priority, so this one runs
/// after the selection translator (50) and the command translator (70), which
/// is why hints they post are already in the stream when it looks. The only
/// translator after it is the final dispatcher, which has the largest
/// priority value.
///
/// The class has no state of its own. Its name is literal: it spies on the
/// stream and informs `TheInGameUI`, and it never creates a message or
/// changes a command. Two kinds of message matter to it:
/// - Hints that exist only for the UI. A mouseover hint says what is under
///   the cursor; a command hint says what order a click would give. The spy
///   passes them to the UI and consumes them, so neither the
///   dispatcher after it nor the command list receives them.
/// - Messages it reports to the UI but keeps: the area-selection hint and the
///   area-selection message (for the drag rectangle), the move, attack-move
///   and force-move orders, the attack and force-attack orders, and the enter
///   order. The message carries on, so an order proceeds normally.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Nothing here is data-driven. The set of messages that count as hints, and
/// the UI function each one calls, is fixed in the translator. Cursor shapes
/// and hint visuals belong to `TheInGameUI` and the mouse code, not to this
/// class.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// What the BFME 2 body shows: the translator forwards the hint and order
/// messages in its dispatch table to eight InGameUI hooks (the begin and end
/// area-select, create move, attack, force-attack, mouseover, command and
/// garrison hint functions), and it destroys the mouseover and command hint
/// messages afterwards. It creates no message of its own.
///
/// It is not the only caller of those hooks. In both games
/// SelectionTranslator::translateGameMessage calls
/// InGameUI::createMouseoverHint and InGameUI::endAreaSelectHint, and the
/// window-input blocking callback (GameWinBlockInput) calls
/// InGameUI::endAreaSelectHint when the left button is released over a
/// blocking window. In BFME 2 InGameUI also calls endAreaSelectHint from one
/// of its own state-reset methods. A replacement UI must therefore implement
/// the hooks themselves, and must not treat this translator as their only
/// caller or replace only the translator. The callers listed are the ones
/// verified (HintSpy, SelectionTranslator, GameWinBlockInput and InGameUI);
/// they are examples, not a complete census, and other callers may exist.
///
/// The hooks carry UI state as well as visuals, so a replacement UI cannot
/// treat them as drawing calls only. BFME 2 evidence, read from the bodies of
/// the hooks themselves:
/// - InGameUI::createMouseoverHint returns at once while the view is scrolling
///   or a selection is in progress. If an opaque window (judged by its status
///   flags) is under the cursor it sets the arrow cursor and returns. For the mouseover-drawable hint it
///   clears the cursor tooltip and records which drawable is under the cursor
///   (the record is skipped when a flag on the object's template says so),
///   builds tooltip text for objects that shroud does not hide and passes it
///   to the mouse code; for the mouseover-location hint it clears the record.
///   It then sets the cursor: in the default mouse mode with nothing selected
///   (during replay playback an extra look-at translator test also applies),
///   the selecting cursor over a selectable drawable that the local player
///   controls and the arrow cursor otherwise; in any other mouse mode, the
///   cursor saved for that mode.
/// - InGameUI::createCommandHint reads that recorded mouse-over drawable and
///   chooses the cursor from the hint message type and the mouse mode, setting
///   it through a helper that does nothing while the view is scrolling or a
///   selection is in progress. It sets the arrow cursor when a window is under
///   the cursor, and in the GUI-command mouse mode it takes the cursor and
///   radius from the pending GUI command.
/// - InGameUI::beginAreaSelectHint sets the drag-select flag and, for a
///   non-null message, stores the message's first argument (a pixel region) as
///   the drag rectangle.
/// - InGameUI::createGarrisonHint looks up the drawable named by the message's
///   first argument and, if it exists, calls a Drawable method on it.
/// - InGameUI::endAreaSelectHint is called from the places listed above,
///   including the UI's input-enable reset, where it sits beside clearing the
///   selecting state and the radius cursor.
///
/// Unconfirmed for BFME 2: the effect of InGameUI::endAreaSelectHint itself
/// (Zero Hour clears the drag-select flag that the W3D in-game UI tests
/// before it draws the drag rectangle), the effects of the move, attack and
/// force-attack hooks (see HintSpyTranslator::translateGameMessage), and
/// whether the Drawable method that createGarrisonHint calls gives the same
/// feedback as Zero Hour's onSelected. Treat these as carrying UI state until
/// they are confirmed.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The dispatch table and its eight InGameUI hooks are read from the BFME 2
/// body. The names of the hooks are Zero Hour's (`HintSpy.cpp` and
/// `InGameUI.cpp` there). The hook set is identical in
/// both games; the message families differ (see
/// HintSpyTranslator::translateGameMessage). The other callers named under
/// Remastering are read from the BFME 2 bodies of
/// SelectionTranslator::translateGameMessage, GameWinBlockInput and the
/// InGameUI state-reset method, and from Zero Hour's `SelectionXlat.cpp` and
/// `GameWindow.cpp`. The descriptions of what the mouseover, command,
/// begin-area-select and garrison hooks do in BFME 2 are read from their BFME 2
/// bodies (Zero Hour's `InGameUI.cpp` is the comparison); the Zero Hour
/// descriptions of the other hooks are Zero Hour's. Which hooks are confirmed
/// from BFME 2 bodies is the evidence read for this page, not a live status;
/// the reverse-engineering ledger and its progress tools show the current
/// state.
/// \see HintSpyTranslator::translateGameMessage, MessageStream::attachTranslator,
///      MessageStream::propagateMessages, GameClient::init


/// \fn GameMessageDisposition HintSpyTranslator::translateGameMessage(const GameMessage *msg)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gameclient_input_credits_sway
/// \brief Calls the in-game UI hook that matches the message and decides
///        whether to remove the message from the stream.
///
/// The message-stream pass calls this for each message still in the stream
/// when the pass reaches this translator; most message types are not in its
/// table and pass untouched with KEEP_MESSAGE.
///
/// \par Dispatch
/// - The two mouseover hints (what drawable is under the cursor, what
///   location): InGameUI::createMouseoverHint, then DESTROY_MESSAGE.
/// - The command-hint family: InGameUI::createCommandHint, then
///   DESTROY_MESSAGE. In Zero Hour these are messages such as the move,
///   attack, enter, dock, repair, hijack, capture and rally-point hints.
///   BFME 2's family is a different set with its own numbering (two blocks
///   of consecutive message types, the larger with a few gaps), and the names
///   of its members are not established. One message number has its own
///   table entry that sets the destroy disposition and then falls into the
///   command-hint case, so it ends in the same action (Zero Hour's defector
///   hint has the same shape; that identification is an inference).
/// - The area-selection hint: InGameUI::beginAreaSelectHint, keeping the
///   message. The area-selection message itself: InGameUI::endAreaSelectHint.
///   Zero Hour's comment says that a hint is always followed by a selection,
///   so the selection ends the hinting.
/// - The move orders (move, attack-move, force-move): InGameUI::createMoveHint.
///   Zero Hour also routes its add-waypoint message here; the BFME 2 table has
///   no such case.
/// - Attack on an object: InGameUI::createAttackHint. Force attack on the
///   ground or on an object: InGameUI::createForceAttackHint. Enter:
///   InGameUI::createGarrisonHint. Each keeps the message.
///
/// \param msg The message being offered. Only its type is read; the hooks
///            read the arguments themselves.
/// \return DESTROY_MESSAGE for the mouseover and command hints, otherwise
///         KEEP_MESSAGE.
///
/// \note The message names above are Zero Hour's, paired with BFME 2's message
/// numbers by position and by the shape of the dispatch table; the numbers
/// themselves were read from the BFME 2 body.
/// \note BFME 2 evidence for the hooks this table calls, read from the BFME 2
/// bodies (details under HintSpyTranslator, Remastering): the mouseover hook
/// returns early while scrolling or selecting, handles window and cursor
/// state, and records the drawable under the cursor; the command hook
/// chooses a cursor from the hint type and the mouse mode; the begin-area-select
/// hook sets the drag flag and region; the garrison hook looks up the drawable
/// and calls a Drawable method on it.
/// \note Zero Hour: the move hint puts a short-lived marker at the destination
/// (and does nothing when the only selected object cannot move), the attack
/// and force-attack hints do nothing, the end-area-select hint clears the
/// drag-select flag, and the garrison hint plays the target drawable's
/// selected feedback. BFME 2 evidence for the move, attack, force-attack and
/// end-area-select hooks themselves is unconfirmed, as is whether the BFME 2
/// garrison hook's Drawable method gives the same feedback.
/// \see HintSpyTranslator, MetaEventTranslator::translateGameMessage


/// \class MetaEventTranslator
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gameclient_input_credits_sway
/// \brief The translator that turns raw keys into the game's meta commands
///        through the key-binding table, and raw mouse buttons into click
///        messages.
///
/// "Raw" messages come straight from the input devices: Keyboard and Mouse
/// append raw key and raw mouse messages to the stream every client frame.
/// The window-system translators (priorities 4, 5 and 10) get the first
/// look. MetaEventTranslator, attached by GameClient::init at priority 20,
/// is the first one that gives raw input a game meaning. It does two jobs:
/// - Key bindings. It matches each raw key-down and key-up against the rows of
///   `TheMetaMap`, the binding table that the `CommandMap` INI blocks fill,
///   and for a hit appends the row's meta message (a command such as "select
///   all" or "stop") to the stream. Later translators act on the meta
///   message; in Zero Hour, for example, the selection translator handles
///   the team-group messages.
/// - Click synthesis. From raw mouse button presses and releases it builds
///   click and double-click messages that carry the dragged-out pixel
///   rectangle and the modifier flags. The selection and command translators
///   work from these, not from the raw button messages.
///
/// The object keeps a small amount of state between messages: the last key
/// pressed, the last modifier state, and for each of the three buttons (left,
/// middle, right) the pixel where it went down and a flag saying that the
/// next release completes a double click.
///
/// \par CommandMap.ini
/// The binding table is data. Each `CommandMap` block is named by one of the
/// meta message names built into the executable (MetaMap::parseMetaMap
/// looks the name up with MetaMap::findGameMessageMetaType and stops with an
/// INI error for an unknown one) and has these fields:
/// - `Key`: a key name from a fixed list of 86 names such as KEY_ESC, KEY_F1
///   or KEY_SPACE.
/// - `Transition`: DOWN or UP. The table also accepts DOUBLEDOWN, but the
///   translator never matches it: only UP and DOWN are tested.
/// - `Modifiers`: NONE, CTRL, ALT, SHIFT, or CTRL_ALT, SHIFT_CTRL,
///   SHIFT_ALT or SHIFT_ALT_CTRL. Left and right keys are not told apart.
/// - `UseableIn`: any of SHELL, GAME and PLANNING (a bit string).
/// - `Category`: CONTROL, INFORMATION, INTERFACE, SELECTION, TAUNT, TEAM,
///   MISC or DEBUG. This translator does not read it.
/// - `Description` and `DisplayName`: localisable labels, which this
///   translator does not read.
/// A row with no `Key` fires on a change of the modifier keys alone.
///
/// MetaMap::getMetaMapRec keeps one row per meta message. A second block
/// naming the same message edits that row field by field instead of adding a
/// second binding, so a message has one key. A new row starts with no key,
/// DOWN, no modifiers, usable in nothing and category MISC, so a block that
/// omits `UseableIn` gives a row that never fires. New rows are put at the
/// head of the list, so the translator reaches them in the reverse of the
/// order in which the INI files first named their messages, and when two rows
/// match the same key event the later-introduced one wins.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which key, modifiers and transition produce which meta
///   message, and in which situations (`UseableIn`), by editing
///   `CommandMap.ini`. The file is loaded without the INI CRC writer, so
///   bindings are per machine and are not compared between players (see
///   \ref page_modding_rules).
/// - Hard-coded: the list of meta message types (a binding can only trigger a
///   type the executable already has, and what that type does is decided by
///   the translators and the logic), the key names, the modifier names, the
///   rule that left and right modifiers are merged, the rule that a binding
///   whose `UseableIn` is exactly GAME does not fire while the client frame
///   is still 0, and the German Y/Z swap described under
///   MetaEventTranslator::translateGameMessage.
/// - A binding creates only a client-side message. Whether it becomes a
///   logic command is up to later translators, so changing bindings changes
///   which messages the local player can produce, not what the logic does
///   with a given command; what a command does after it reaches the logic is
///   outside this page.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - This is the layer to replace for input remapping, controllers or layout
///   awareness. Its inputs are the engine's raw key and mouse messages, in
///   which keys are DirectInput scan codes; its outputs are the meta and click
///   messages, which the rest of the client consumes unchanged.
/// - Click detection is split: the mouse device layer raises the raw
///   double-click messages, and this class pairs them with the next release.
///   The tolerance below which a drag counts as a point click is read from the
///   mouse object.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The BFME 2 body and the parse tables are the facts. The role of each
/// field, the meaning of the four state members and the click synthesis are
/// the same as Zero Hour's (`MetaEvent.cpp`), which the BFME 2 body follows
/// except for the differences listed under
/// MetaEventTranslator::translateGameMessage.
/// \see MetaEventTranslator::translateGameMessage, MetaMap::parseMetaMap,
///      MessageStream::attachTranslator, Keyboard::createStreamMessages,
///      Mouse::createStreamMessages


/// \fn GameMessageDisposition MetaEventTranslator::translateGameMessage(const GameMessage *msg)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gameclient_input_credits_sway
/// \brief Maps a raw key to a bound meta command, or builds a click message
///        from a raw mouse release.
///
/// Called by the message-stream pass for each message. Messages other than
/// raw key down and up and the raw mouse button messages are ignored and
/// kept.
///
/// \par Keys
/// For a raw key message (argument 0 is the scan code, argument 1 the key
/// state flags):
/// - With the global language set to German (value 2 in Zero Hour's
///   numbering), the Y and Z scan codes are swapped first.
///   Keyboard::initKeyNames sets that language when Windows reports a German
///   keyboard layout (other code may set it as well), so
///   a binding written for the Y key follows the letter printed on a QWERTZ
///   keyboard. Zero Hour has no such swap.
/// - The state flags are reduced to three modifier flags (control, shift and
///   alt), merging left and right.
/// - The situations the game is in are reduced to a mask: the shell bit when
///   the shell is showing, otherwise the game bit, with a third bit added
///   (the mask is then 6) when a member of the local player object equals 2.
///   Zero Hour has no such bit. That the member is planning mode, and so that
///   the extra bit means "planning", is an unverified inference. What
///   supports it: the `UseableIn` name table has `PLANNING` as its third name,
///   so the third bit of the mask is named that way in the `CommandMap.ini`
///   syntax, and retail binds Apt callbacks named
///   `AptPalantir::OnPlanningModeUILoaded` and
///   `AptPalantir::OnPlanningModeUIUnloaded`, so a planning-mode UI exists.
///   Neither fact shows that the player member holds the planning state.
/// - The binding rows are walked in list order and the first row that fires
///   ends the walk. A row is skipped if its `UseableIn` is exactly GAME and
///   the client frame is still 0 (the test is an equality, so a row with GAME
///   plus another bit is not skipped; Zero Hour's comment for the same test
///   says it keeps in-game commands from running during a map load), or if
///   its `UseableIn` bits share nothing with the mask. A remaining row
///   fires in one of two ways:
///   - a modifier-only row (no `Key`) when the modifier state has changed,
///     and either it is an UP row whose modifiers equal the previous state or
///     a DOWN row whose modifiers equal the new state;
///   - a key row when the key matches, the modifiers match exactly, and the
///     transition matches the event (UP rows on key-up, DOWN rows on key-down).
///   A key row appends its meta message to the stream unless the key event is
///   an auto-repeat, and consumes the raw key either way, except when a flag
///   on the Living World object is set, in which case the key is kept. What
///   that flag means is not established. The body reads the flag without
///   testing the Living World object for null.
/// - A modifier-only row always appends and consumes.
/// - Afterwards it remembers the key (on a key-down) and the modifier state.
///
/// \par Mouse buttons
/// - On a button press: remembers the pixel and clears the double-click
///   flag for that button.
/// - On a raw double-click message: sets the flag for that button.
/// - On a release: inserts, directly after the raw release in the stream, a
///   left, middle or right click message, or the double-click version if the
///   flag was set, and clears the flag. The new message gets two
///   arguments: the pixel rectangle from the remembered press position to
///   the release position, and the modifier integer of the raw message. If
///   both sides of the rectangle are smaller than the mouse's drag tolerance,
///   it is collapsed to a single point, which is how a slightly shaky click
///   still counts as a click. The raw release itself is kept.
///
/// \param msg The message being offered; only raw key and raw mouse button
///            messages do anything.
/// \return DESTROY_MESSAGE when a key was consumed, otherwise KEEP_MESSAGE.
///
/// \note Zero Hour also had a special case here for the replay fast-forward
/// meta message. On a non-repeating press of a key row bound to it, when the
/// game is a replay game (a debug-cheat build define removes that condition),
/// it toggled the fast-forward mode itself and showed a message, because,
/// per Zero Hour's comment, the translator that normally handles that message
/// is disabled during cinematics. Whether or not it toggled, it appended no
/// meta message and kept the raw key. The BFME 2 body has no such case.
/// \note An auto-repeated key that matches a binding produces no new meta
/// message. In Zero Hour it is always consumed; in BFME 2 it is consumed
/// unless the Living World flag described under Keys is set, in which case
/// the key is kept.
/// \see MetaEventTranslator, SelectionTranslator::translateGameMessage


/// \class WindowTranslator
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gameclient_input_credits_sway
/// \brief The translator that offers raw mouse and key messages to the
///        legacy window system and consumes whatever a window uses.
///
/// The legacy window system (`TheWindowManager`, a tree of `GameWindow`
/// objects with input callbacks) is the older of the game's two UI layers; the
/// Apt layer has its own translator and window manager (\ref sub_gui_apt).
/// WindowTranslator is how the legacy system receives input. It converts a raw
/// mouse message into a window message (rawMouseToWindowMessage), calls
/// the window manager's mouse or key entry, and, if a window reports that it
/// used the input, destroys the message so that nothing later (selection,
/// orders) also reacts to a click that landed on a button.
///
/// \par Two instances, two modes
/// The class has one member of its own, a mode number, and
/// WindowTranslator::translateGameMessage behaves differently for mode 0
/// and mode 1. GameClient::init attaches two translators that run this same
/// body:
/// - an instance made by a default constructor that leaves the mode at 0, at
///   priority 4, ahead of the Apt translator at priority 5;
/// - an instance made by WindowTranslator's own constructor, which sets the
///   mode to 1, at priority 10, after the Apt translator.
/// Both instances use the same virtual table in retail, which is why one
/// body serves both. Zero Hour has a single, modeless translator at
/// priority 10.
///
/// In outline, a message that no window uses in the first pass is offered to
/// the window manager again in the second. Before each offer the translator
/// stores its mode in the window manager, which suggests that the manager
/// selects windows by pass; what the manager does with the value is not
/// established by this body, so treat that as an inference.
/// - Mode 0 is the plain pass. For a mouse message it first gives the
///   pixel position to the Apt window manager, so the Apt UI is told where
///   the cursor is for every mouse message this instance sees. It does not look at the shell or the in-game
///   UI's input switch.
/// - Mode 1 is the gated pass. It also consumes mouse messages while the
///   shell is showing or the in-game UI has input switched off, lets the
///   Escape key stop a playing movie when the global data allows it, and
///   swallows Escape while input is off.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Nothing in this class is data-driven. Which windows receive input, and what
/// they do with it, is up to the layouts and callbacks of the windows
/// themselves (\ref sub_gui_apt); the rules listed for the two modes are
/// fixed in code.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// This class and the Apt translator are the places where raw input meets the
/// two UI systems. Mouse positions are integer pixels in the game's
/// resolution, and key events carry DirectInput scan codes plus state flags.
/// A port with different input devices or UI scaling would change the raw
/// producers and, if windows are laid out at a different scale, map positions
/// here or in the window manager. Because the translator hands its mode to the
/// window manager, the two have to be ported together.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The body, the constructor and the attachment priorities are BFME 2
/// evidence. The name and the role come from Zero Hour (`WindowXlat.cpp`).
/// The mode scheme and the Apt position hand-off have no Zero Hour
/// counterpart; their purpose is inferred from the body.
/// \see WindowTranslator::translateGameMessage, rawMouseToWindowMessage,
///      AptTranslator::translateGameMessage, GameClient::init


/// \fn GameMessageDisposition WindowTranslator::translateGameMessage(const GameMessage *msg)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gameclient_input_credits_sway
/// \brief Offers a raw mouse or key message to the window system and destroys
///        it if a window used it.
///
/// Called once for each of the two instances, so a message that the first
/// instance destroys is not offered to the second (see WindowTranslator).
/// Message types other than the raw mouse and key messages are kept.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// 1. Stores the instance's mode in the window manager, then converts the
///    message with rawMouseToWindowMessage. That conversion is for mouse
///    messages and gives "none" for the rest.
/// 2. Early exits that keep the message:
///    - Mode 1: while the Living World object's flag is set, the game is not
///      paused and the shell is not showing, nothing is processed. What the
///      flag means is not established.
///    - Mode 0: for a mouse message, the pixel position goes to the Apt window
///      manager first. Then, if either of two global gates is set (neither is
///      identified), nothing more is processed.
///    - Both modes: if the tactical view reports its mouse as locked,
///      nothing is processed. Zero Hour lets the left button through while
///      the interface is scrolling, so a click on the control bar is not
///      lost; the BFME 2 body has no such exception.
/// 3. Dispatch on the message type:
///    - Mouse position, button down, button up and double click: the pixel
///      position goes to the window manager's mouse entry with the converted
///      window message. While the in-game UI reports a placement as anchored,
///      a left-button release is not destroyed even if a window used it. The
///      reason is not established: Zero Hour's only comment on the same line
///      reads "If we release the button outside". In mode 1, unless the Living
///      World flag is set and the game is not paused, the result is forced to
///      "used" while the shell is showing or the in-game UI's input is off.
///    - Left, middle or right drag: the position and the drag delta go to the
///      mouse entry. Mode 1 forces "used" in the same two situations.
///    - Wheel: the position and the wheel value go to the mouse entry, with the
///      same mode 1 rule.
///    - Key down and up: the key code and state go to the window manager's
///      key entry. In mode 1, a released Escape stops a playing movie, if the
///      global data allows that, and counts as used; and a released Escape
///      counts as used while the in-game UI's input is off.
/// 4. If the result is "used" and the message was not forced to be kept, the
///    message is destroyed.
///
/// \param msg The message being offered. Mouse messages carry the pixel
///            position in argument 0; drags carry the delta and wheel
///            messages the wheel value in argument 1; key messages carry the
///            key code and the state flags in arguments 0 and 1.
/// \return DESTROY_MESSAGE if a window (or one of the mode 1 rules) used the
///         input and the message was not forced to be kept, otherwise
///         KEEP_MESSAGE.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's body also has a case for the attack-move toggle meta
/// message, which asks the window manager to refresh its notion of the
/// mouse position and keeps the message; the BFME 2 body has no such case.
/// The shell and disabled-input rules, the movie escape and the Escape rule
/// are unconditional in Zero Hour.
/// \see WindowTranslator, rawMouseToWindowMessage


/// \file GameClient/MessageStream/WindowXlat.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gameclient_input_credits_sway
/// \brief The window-system translator and the raw-mouse helper that it and
///        the Apt translator share.


/// \fn GameWindowMessage rawMouseToWindowMessage(const GameMessage *msg)
/// \brief Converts a raw mouse message into the window system's message code.
///
/// A pure lookup, used by WindowTranslator::translateGameMessage and by
/// AptTranslator::translateGameMessage (in Zero Hour the function is private
/// to the window translator's file).
///
/// \par Mapping
/// - Raw mouse position: the window mouse-position message.
/// - Left, middle or right button down, and also their double-click messages:
///   the matching "down" message. The window system does not distinguish a
///   double click from a press, so the second press of a double click
///   arrives as a plain down message.
/// - Left, middle or right button up: the matching "up" message.
/// - Left, middle or right drag: the matching "drag" message.
/// - Wheel: "wheel up" if argument 1 of the message is greater than zero,
///   otherwise "wheel down". A wheel value of zero therefore counts as down.
/// - Anything else, such as a key message, gives "none".
///
/// \param msg The raw message; its type, and for a wheel message the integer
///            in argument 1, are read.
/// \return The window message code, or "none".
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Both enumerations (the raw mouse message types and the window message
/// codes) have Zero Hour's numbering in BFME 2: the retail jump table covers
/// the same range with the same unhandled cases, and the returned codes
/// match Zero Hour's list counted from "none". The mapping is Zero Hour's
/// code, which the BFME 2 body repeats.
/// \see WindowTranslator::translateGameMessage


/// \file GameClient/SelectionInfoContextCommand.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gameclient_input_credits_sway
/// \brief contextCommandForNewSelection, the selection-versus-command
///        decision of the selection translator.


/// \fn Bool contextCommandForNewSelection(const DrawableList *currentlySelectedDrawables, const DrawableList *newlySelectedDrawables, SelectionInfo *outSelectionInfo, Bool selectionIsPoint)
/// \brief Decides whether a click or drag over some drawables should be
///        carried out as a context-sensitive command rather than as a new
///        selection, and counts what is selected and what is under the
///        cursor.
///
/// When the player clicks or drags in the world, the same gesture could mean
/// "select what is under here" or "give an order to the selected units"
/// (attack that enemy, enter that building). The
/// selection translator calls this function from its handler for the
/// left-click message, after it has collected the drawables under the click
/// or the drag rectangle. If the
/// result is true, the selection translator leaves the selection alone and
/// the gesture is left to the command side of the stream. If it is false, the
/// gesture is a selection and the translator goes on to decide how to select,
/// using the counts this function leaves in the output structure.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Returns false at once if any pointer is null, or if the in-game UI is in
///   force-attack mode or force-move mode.
/// - Counting pass over the currently selected drawables, skipping any
///   without an object: an object the local player controls adds to "mine";
///   if it is infantry it also adds to "mine infantry", and otherwise, if it
///   is a structure, to "mine buildings". Any other object is classified by
///   the local player's relationship to its team: allies are "friends",
///   enemies are "enemies" and neutrals are "civilians".
/// - Counting pass over the newly selected drawables: for each object, a
///   garrisonable building (as `ActionManager::canPlayerGarrison` judges it
///   for the local player) and a crate (by kind) are counted; then "mine"
///   (also counting "mine buildings"), friends, enemies and civilians as above,
///   remembering the last drawable of each group.
/// - If the current selection holds any enemy, friend or civilian, there is no
///   context command: false.
/// - Alternate mouse (a player option): BFME 2 returns false when the UI has
///   no pending GUI command (the accessor's name in Zero Hour's InGameUI), or
///   when that command's type is one particular value (10) whose meaning in
///   BFME 2 is not established. Zero Hour returns false unconditionally in
///   this mode.
/// - If the current selection holds none of the player's own objects, false.
///   Otherwise, in this order:
///   - new enemies under the cursor: with exactly one enemy and a point
///     click, the result is whether `GameClient::evaluateContextCommand`,
///     called for that drawable and its position with the constant 2 (the
///     evaluate-only mode; see Remastering), returns a nonzero value.
///     Otherwise the result is just `selectionIsPoint`.
///   - new objects of the player's own: with exactly one, a point click, and
///     prefer-selection mode off, the same evaluate call's nonzero result for
///     that drawable; otherwise false.
///   - new friends: one friend and a point click, the same evaluate call's
///     nonzero result; otherwise false.
///   - infantry currently selected and exactly one garrisonable building
///     under the cursor: true.
///   - new civilians: one civilian and a point click, the same evaluate
///     call's nonzero result; otherwise false.
///   - new crates: true only for exactly one crate and a point click.
/// - Otherwise the result is `selectionIsPoint`.
///
/// \param currentlySelectedDrawables The drawables selected now.
/// \param newlySelectedDrawables The drawables that the gesture would select.
/// \param[in,out] outSelectionInfo Counters for both groups. The function
///        only increments them, so the caller must pass a zeroed structure
///        (Zero Hour's constructor does that). The "select" flags in the
///        structure are for the caller and are not touched here.
/// \param selectionIsPoint True for a click, false for a drag; the selection
///        translator takes it from a pixel rectangle of zero width and
///        height, which MetaEventTranslator::translateGameMessage produces
///        for a click that moved less than the drag tolerance.
/// \return True if the gesture should be handled as a context command.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The outcome follows data that mods already control: the kind-of flags of
/// object templates (infantry, structure, crate), team relationships, and
/// whether a building can be garrisoned. The decision rules themselves are
/// fixed, including the "exactly one target" requirement. The alternate mouse
/// setting is a player option.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// It runs on the client. In Zero Hour the evaluate-only mode asks whether a
/// command would apply without creating one. BFME 2 has the same three modes
/// with the same numbering (command 0, hint 1, evaluate-only 2, which is the
/// constant this function passes): GameClient::evaluateContextCommand
/// forwards to CommandTranslator::evaluateContextCommand, or returns the
/// invalid message type when the client has no command translator. In that
/// body the evaluate-only mode shares the branches of the command mode for
/// most orders. The branches that append an order message inline test for the
/// command mode first (hint mode appends hint messages instead), and the
/// enter, move-to-location and attack helpers that receive the mode return a
/// message type without appending an order message when called in
/// evaluate-only mode.
///
/// Hint messages are a different matter. Some branches of the evaluator test
/// only for the command mode, or not at all, before appending a hint message,
/// so an evaluate-only call can append hint messages to the stream in those
/// branches. Three examples: the branch for a pending GUI command that is not
/// currently valid appends a hint message whatever the mode; a branch whose
/// command-mode path issues a special-power order tests only for the command
/// mode, so its else path serves the hint and evaluate-only modes alike; and
/// a branch chosen by an attack-result value appends a hint message with no
/// mode test. These are examples, not a
/// complete list of such paths. Treat evaluate-only as not guaranteed free of
/// stream effects.
///
/// This is a local selection decision: the return value decides whether the
/// gesture is left to the command translator, and the orders that result from
/// that are what the network carries. Anything that changes the shape of a
/// click (touch or controller input) needs to keep the point-versus-drag
/// distinction that this function takes as a flag.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The decision table is Zero Hour's (`SelectionInfo.cpp`); the BFME 2 body
/// repeats it, with the alternate-mouse test as the one change read from the
/// body. The kind-of tests and the structure of the output record were read
/// from the BFME 2 body; the meaning of the command type 10 is not known.
/// What the evaluate-only mode means (asks whether a command would apply
/// without creating one) is carried from Zero Hour's `CommandTranslator`
/// (`EVALUATE_ONLY` in `CommandXlat.cpp`). In BFME 2 the evaluate-only
/// enumerator also equals 2, and the BFME 2 body passes that constant to the
/// game client's evaluate-context-command entry through a virtual slot. The
/// Remastering remarks on BFME 2's evaluate path come from the BFME 2 bodies
/// of that entry, of CommandTranslator::evaluateContextCommand and of the
/// enter, move-to-location and attack helpers; the helper names are Zero
/// Hour's.
/// \see SelectionTranslator::translateGameMessage, GameClient::evaluateContextCommand,
///      ActionManager::canPlayerGarrison
